7. To implement thread based image processing application in Microsoft Azure
- Create an Azure Account and open the Azure Portal.
- Search for Storage Accounts, click Create, enter a name (e.g., imagestorage123), choose a region, and click Create.
- Open the Storage Account, go to Containers, click + Container, name it "images" with Private access, and click Create.
- Open the "images" container, click Upload, select multiple image files, and upload them.
- Go to Access Keys in the Storage Account and copy the Connection String.
- Install required libraries on your system using: pip install azure-storage-blob pillow.
- Write the multithreaded Python code (inserting your connection string) and run it using python app.py:
    import threading
    from azure.storage.blob import BlobServiceClient
    from PIL import Image
    import io

    connection_string = "YOUR_CONNECTION_STRING"
    container_name = "images"
    blob_service_client = BlobServiceClient.from_connection_string(connection_string)

    def process_image(blob_name):
        blob_client = blob_service_client.get_blob_client(container=container_name, blob=blob_name)
        data = blob_client.download_blob().readall()
        stream = io.BytesIO(data)
        img = Image.open(stream)
        img = img.resize((200, 200)) 
        output = io.BytesIO()
        img.save(output, format='JPEG')
        output.seek(0)
        new_name = "processed_" + blob_name
        blob_service_client.get_blob_client(container=container_name, blob=new_name).upload_blob(output, overwrite=True)

    def main():
        container_client = blob_service_client.get_container_client(container_name)
        blobs = container_client.list_blobs()
        threads = []
        for blob in blobs:
            t = threading.Thread(target=process_image, args=(blob.name,))
            threads.append(t)
            t.start()
        for t in threads:
            t.join()
        print("All images processed successfully")

    if __name__ == "__main__":
        main()
- Verify output by checking the Azure Blob Container for files prefixed with processed_.

8. To deploy dynamic web application on EC2 Instance on AWS
- Type EC2 in the search box in AWS, click on EC2, and select Launch instance.
- Under Name and tags, give it a name like "web server".
- Under Application and OS images, select Amazon Linux AWS.
- Select t3.micro under Instance type.
- Create a new key pair (e.g., kyp), download the .pem file, and proceed.
- In network settings, enable allowing SSH, HTTPS, and HTTP traffic from the internet.
- Launch the instance, go to Instances, select your new instance, and click Connect using EC2 Instance Connect.
- Execute the following commands in the terminal:
    sudo su -
    yum update -y
    yum install -y httpd
    mkdir temp
    cd temp
    wget https://templatemo.com/download/templatemo_596_electric_xtra
    mkdir templatemo_596_electric_xtra_unzipped
    unzip templatemo_596_electric_xtra -d templatemo_596_electric_xtra_unzipped
    cd templatemo_596_electric_xtra_unzipped
    cd templatemo_596_electric_xtra
    mv * /var/www/html/
    cd /var/www/html/
    systemctl start httpd
    systemctl enable httpd
    systemctl status httpd
- Copy the public IPv4 address of the instance, paste it into a browser, and view the web page.

9. To use Google App Engine Launcher to launch web applications.
- Create a sub-folder named ae--01--trivial inside an apps directory on your desktop.
- Create a file named app.yaml in this folder with the following contents:
    application: ae-01-trivial
    version: 1
    runtime: python
    api_version: 1
    handlers:
    - url: /.*
      script: index.py
- Create a file named index.py in the same folder with the following contents:
    print 'Content-Type: text/plain'
    print ''
    print 'Hello there Chuck'
- Start the GoogleAppEngineLauncher program.
- Use File --> Add Existing Application and select the ae--01--trivial folder.
- Select the application in the launcher and press Run.
- Press Browse or navigate to http://localhost:8080/ in a browser to see the application.
- Check the Logs button in the launcher to view internal server actions and troubleshoot errors.

10. To deploy static web application using S3 on AWS and secure it with signed URL’s
- Go to S3 in the AWS console, click create bucket, enter a name, and uncheck "Block all public access".
- Select "Properties" in the bucket, scroll to the bottom, edit Static website hosting, enable it, and specify index.html for both index and error documents.
- Go to "Permissions", edit the Bucket Policy, replace BUCKET_NAME with your bucket's name, and save the following JSON:
    {
        "Version": "2012-10-17",
        "Statement": [
            {
                "Sid": "PublicReadGetObject",
                "Effect": "Allow",
                "Principal": "*",
                "Action": "s3:*",
                "Resource": [
                    "arn:aws:s3:::BUCKET_NAME/*",
                    "arn:aws:s3:::BUCKET_NAME"
                ]
            }
        ]
    }
- Upload your static website files (e.g., index.html, styles.css) into the bucket.
- Go to CloudFront, click Create Distribution, and paste your S3 bucket's static URL into the Origin Domain (removing https://).
- Set Viewer protocol policy to "Redirect HTTP to HTTPS" and click Create Distribution.
- Access your website using the generated Distribution domain name.

11. Create Video streaming service using S3 and cloudfront and AWS Elemental Media Convert Digital Rights Management System
- Navigate to S3 in the AWS Console and create a bucket with a globally unique name.
- Ensure "Block all public access" remains checked, enable Bucket Versioning, and enable Default Encryption with SSE-S3.
- Upload a test .mp4 video to the new S3 bucket.
- Open the CloudFront console and click Create distribution.
- Select your S3 bucket as the Origin domain, select "Origin access control settings (recommended)" for Origin access, and click Create control setting.
- Set Viewer protocol policy to "Redirect HTTP to HTTPS" and Cache policy to "CachingOptimized".
- Click Create distribution and copy the policy JSON provided in the banner at the top of the screen.
- Return to the S3 bucket, go to the Permissions tab, edit the Bucket policy, paste the copied CloudFront policy, and save.
- Test the stream by opening a browser and appending the uploaded video file's name to the end of the CloudFront Distribution domain URL.
